WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.999 --> 00:00:04.160
Before we get into today’s episode I wanted
to let you know that we’re going to be taking

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.080
a short break from uploading videos for the
next couple of weeks, but we will be back

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.300
soon with brand new episodes.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.490
And you won’t have to miss us the entire
time because some of the Microcosmos team

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.720
will be doing a livestream here on the channel
during the break, and you can find more information

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.297
about that on the community tab here on YouTube
or on our twitter @journeytomicro.

00:00:42.921 --> 00:00:48.500
One day, James—our master of microscopes—was
cleaning the marine tanks that some of his

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.668
organisms live in when he noticed this creature.

00:00:54.173 --> 00:00:58.980
It was hard to miss given that it was visible
to the naked eye, thanks to both its bright

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.383
red color and large size.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.820
What he had found was a species of foraminifera,
which are single-celled amoeboid creatures

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:13.380
that spend their lives poking their false
feet—or pseudopodia—through holes in their

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.610
tests, which is what we call their shells.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:22.750
But foraminifera are perhaps best known for
what they reveal to us in death, their tests

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:29.460
are preserved as fossils that record our planet’s
history in their own chemical composition.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:36.031
And among the various things that make foraminifera
so helpful is one very simple fact: foraminifera

00:01:36.031 --> 00:01:38.650
fossils are large.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.860
For comparison, here is a one Euro coin sitting
next to an array of foraminifera fossils.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.890
The forams are not large the way a
T. rex is large, but they are still big enough

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.500
to substitute for an object that people use
every day.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.159
When you zoom in to our giant red foraminifera,
you can see the contents of its cytoplasm

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:04.810
streaming in both directions.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.509
We think that this particular foram is a member
of a group called xenophyophores, which are

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:15.405
deep-sea organisms that have tiny crystals
in their cytoplasm called granellae that are

00:02:15.405 --> 00:02:21.185
usually made from barium sulfite absorbed
like a glittery souvenir from the places the

00:02:21.185 --> 00:02:23.507
foram once lived.

00:02:23.507 --> 00:02:28.000
Usually when we look at slides under the microscope,
we’re on the hunt for creatures that are

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.450
smaller than a speck of dust, creatures for
whom the whole microscopy slide is a vast

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.550
world they may never see the entirety of.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.610
But this foraminifera is large.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.560
Yes, we are aware that we have said that
multiple times in this video, but it's really

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.040
worth emphasizing.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.420
The coverslip that James used for this footage
was about 22 millimeters in length on each

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:56.260
side, and our mysterious red giant managed
to extend its web of pseudopodia across practically

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:58.281
the entire surface.

00:02:58.281 --> 00:03:02.276
And yet, we don’t want you to think that
all foraminifera are like this.

00:03:02.276 --> 00:03:07.489
Over 500 million years, there have been tens
of thousands of foraminifera species both

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:10.580
extinct and still surviving that we know of.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:17.030
There some are far less than a millimeter in
diameter, while others are centimeters in diameter.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.214
The larger of these foraminifera are known
by the extremely technical name of

00:03:21.214 --> 00:03:22.905
“larger foraminifera.”

00:03:22.905 --> 00:03:27.020
It’s a name that hints at a question we’ve
sometimes wondered during our journey through

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:33.959
the microcosmos: If life works so well at
such tiny scales, why bother with size?

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:36.478
What is the point of being large?

00:03:39.556 --> 00:03:43.620
Of course this question is too big for 
any single organism to answer.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.010
But scientists have spent a considerable amount
of time studying foraminifera, which has, yes,

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.200
led to the publication of papers 
with relatable titles like

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.568
"Why are larger foraminifera large?”

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.380
Larger foraminifera are typically found in
warm waters where conditions are stable, but

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.977
food is usually scarce.

00:04:06.977 --> 00:04:10.580
That might sound surprising: it seems like
if you’re living in an area without much

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:14.030
food, it would be difficult to grow so large.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:20.602
But one of the reasons these particular foraminifera
are able to grow so large is because they have help.

00:04:20.602 --> 00:04:26.140
Within their tests are chambers that house
algal symbionts like the foraminifera’s

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:31.050
own personal greenhouse that they can harvest
photosynthetic products from.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:36.123
These symbionts compensate for the lack of
nutrition in the foraminifera’s environment.

00:04:36.123 --> 00:04:42.167
And yet symbiosis alone is not enough to explain
the larger foraminifera’s size.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:47.050
There are smaller species with algae symbionts,
and larger species without them.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.530
So while the symbionts help the organisms
survive in these nutrient-poor areas—and

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:56.370
it certainly doesn’t hurt to have the size
and test complexity to house them—they aren’t

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.860
the main factor that decides that a foram
species will become larger.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.690
What ultimately drives that shift is the choice
foraminifera make on when to reproduce.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:12.189
In an environment where food is plentiful,
forams will reproduce at an earlier age.

00:05:12.189 --> 00:05:17.110
But reproducing earlier means smaller baby
forams that are vulnerable to predators

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:21.080
or currents that take them to less ideal environments.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.770
So these smaller foraminifera are playing
a numbers game, their survival as a species

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.740
is contingent on proliferating enough that these
threats to the individual forams can’t

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.600
take them all out.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.570
But larger foraminifera aren’t living in
those kinds of places.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:43.070
They brave more restricted conditions, ones
where it makes more sense for them to wait

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:48.620
a little longer to reproduce so they can focus
their resources and energy on producing offspring

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:54.240
that will be more likely to survive than a
large population of tiny foraminifera.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.539
These larger forams spend years expanding
their single-celled bodies, so that by the

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:03.681
time they reproduce, their offspring will
be larger and hardier

00:06:03.681 --> 00:06:06.199
compared to their smaller counterparts.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:11.639
Their survival as a species is driven by the
survival of these individual young.

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:17.732
And over millions of generations, this strategy
means the evolution of a larger species.

00:06:17.732 --> 00:06:23.639
These adaptations have made the large foraminifera
very specialized in terms of the kind of conditions

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:24.639
they thrive in.

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:30.130
And that’s great for survival in the moment,
but it creates problems for them in the very,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:31.940
very, very long term.

00:06:32.373 --> 00:06:36.199
In the hundreds of millions of years that
foraminifera have been around, mass extinctions

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:41.464
have wiped out countless species—including,
it turns out, larger foraminifera.

00:06:41.464 --> 00:06:46.740
Again and again, their adaptation to such
a narrow, specialized environment has rendered

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.760
them unable to contend with the changes around
them fast enough.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.960
And on the flip side of the extinction coin,
their smaller, more rapidly reproducing counterparts

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.427
have had the numbers and adaptability to survive.

00:07:01.427 --> 00:07:06.120
They may not be as well-adapted to the same
niche that larger foraminifera are, but

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:12.020
what good is being well adapted to a niche 
when that niche is changing?

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:17.477
Through the fossil record left behind by foraminifera,
we can see this history play out.

00:07:17.477 --> 00:07:24.228
Over and over again, the fossils show, mass
extinctions have obliterated larger foraminifera.

00:07:24.228 --> 00:07:29.889
But the fact that it happens again and again
also reveals something else: the larger foraminifera

00:07:29.889 --> 00:07:35.880
may find themselves knocked down with each
extinction, but…well, they get back up again.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.947
It seems like evolution is never gonna keep
them down.

00:07:39.947 --> 00:07:42.288
Because that’s what happens: evolution.

00:07:42.288 --> 00:07:48.319
Life rebounds, and when it does, the larger
foraminifera evolve once again, in the same

00:07:48.319 --> 00:07:51.454
kinds of conditions we see them living in
today.

00:07:51.454 --> 00:07:58.388
Over and over again, this form appears, almost
as if the larger foraminifera is inevitable.

00:07:59.008 --> 00:08:01.755
But of course, nothing is inevitable.

00:08:01.755 --> 00:08:06.050
Life has gone through so many extinctions,
and much of what has emerged on the other

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:09.346
side is different from what came before.

00:08:09.346 --> 00:08:15.509
We live with the shadows of dinosaurs and
giant mammals, and maybe the future could

00:08:15.509 --> 00:08:18.979
some day hold  some shadow form of us.

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:25.169
And in that future, foraminifera fossils
will record the world as we lived it, a world

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:31.940
that the larger foraminifera probably did
not survive, but that it will still have managed

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.212
to return to, eventually.

00:08:40.825 --> 00:08:46.039
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

